WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
MFA methods provide different levels of assurance.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.240
One-time passcodes from authenticator apps are common and familiar.

3
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:12.220
Push approvals can be convenient but need careful monitoring and user clarity.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:23.040
SMS and email codes may be useful in some environments, but they are often weaker options because they depend on communication channels and account recovery paths outside the application.

5
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:30.020
Hardware security keys and passkeys can provide stronger resistance to some phishing and replay risks when implemented correctly.

6
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:33.500
Authenticator strength is not only a technical label.

7
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:41.760
It includes resistance to phishing, replay, social engineering, device loss, operational failure, and user confusion.

8
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:51.860
A method that is strong on paper may still fail if enrollment is weak, recovery is easy to abuse, support teams bypass the process, or users cannot understand what they are approving.

9
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.320
Security and usability have to be designed together.

10
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.640
Backup codes are an important recovery mechanism, not a casual convenience.

11
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:09.780
They can provide an alternate path into an account when the primary factor is unavailable, so they should be generated, displayed, stored, used, and retired carefully.

12
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:23.080
Biometrics at a high level often act as a local unlock mechanism for a device or authenticator, so applications should understand what proof they actually receive rather than assuming the biometric itself is sent to the server.

13
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.200
Choosing MFA methods requires matching risk to context.

14
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:37.300
Consumer accounts, internal tools, privileged administration, financial actions, health data access, and service operations may need different assurance levels.

15
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.320
Accessibility also matters.

16
00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.180
Strong MFA should not lock out legitimate users unnecessarily.

17
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:51.920
The durable design principle is to choose authenticators that fit the risk, protect enrollment and reset, and give users a clear and supportable path through the process.
